skip
BOUNDED OBSERVATION, no graded answer -- and its own header says so at :1. It runs the
@experimental bot on real River Zeta for a fixed ~100s window so the offense module's
[exp-reach] and [exp-offense] lines accumulate in debug.log, then exits so the agent can
read them. The "behavior bar" in the header (at least one non-central axis visible) is
addressed to that reader; the Lua evaluates nothing.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. Until 2026-09-01 the terminal verdict was `Test.Pass`, so this
scenario contributed a green to every `run-batch.sh --all` tally regardless of what the
log showed. `Test.Skip` is the harness's own word for "ran, produced no graded answer"
(TestGlobal.cs) and run-batch.sh maps it to the SKIP bucket. The scenario is not hidden
and still runs; the verdict just stops claiming an answer it never computed.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever turns the header's behavior bar into a
Lua predicate. A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades STALE/RED on purpose: a
scenario that STARTS grading shows up loudly instead of silently changing meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail`
declarations, where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green.
A timeout reaches expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED --
which matters here, because this scenario runs a full bot match and is one of the more
plausible candidates for a hang.
